{% comment %}
=====================================================================
 One document for the whole set — counted in SQL, not in Liquid
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 Set the template's OUTPUT to "One document for the whole set" and its
 SOURCE to "Issues".

 THE ONE THING WORTH LEARNING FROM THIS FILE: `{% sql_aggregate %}`
 counts in the database. It does not load the issues, so a report over
 40 000 issues costs the same as one over 40 — and the numbers are
 computed over exactly the issues the reader is allowed to see.

 A `for issue in issues` loop is the alternative and it is the
 wrong tool for a total: it reads every row to add up numbers the
 database can add up itself.

 (Written without its Liquid tag delimiters on purpose. On Liquid 4 a
 comment block still tokenises its body, and a BLOCK tag — for, if, unless,
 case, capture, tablerow, raw — written inside a comment opens a block that
 then meets the end of the comment, and the whole template fails to parse.
 A simple tag such as sql_aggregate above is safe, because a comment ignores
 a tag it does not know. Liquid 5 stopped reading comment bodies altogether,
 which is why this cost a release to find. See T-42.)

 `drill: true` puts a URL on every bucket, so every row below is a link
 into the issue list filtered to that row. It works in the PDF as well —
 those are real link annotations, not blue text.
=====================================================================
{% endcomment %}

{% sql_aggregate from: issues, group_by: status, drill: true, assign_to: by_status %}
{% sql_aggregate from: issues, group_by: priority, drill: true, assign_to: by_priority %}

<h1>Issue report — {{ project.name }}</h1>
<p class="rrd-muted">{{ by_status.total }} issues in scope</p>

<div class="rrd-card">
  <h2>By status</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Status</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Issues</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for bucket in by_status.buckets %}
        <tr>
          <td>
            {% comment %} `bucket.url` is nil for a row that cannot be expressed as an
               issue-list filter, so the link is conditional rather than assumed — a link
               to nowhere is worse than a plain label. {% endcomment %}
            {% if bucket.url %}
              <a href="{{ bucket.url }}">{{ bucket.label }}</a>
            {% else %}
              {{ bucket.label }}
            {% endif %}
          </td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ bucket.count }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
    <tfoot>
      <tr>
        <th>Total</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">{{ by_status.total }}</th>
      </tr>
    </tfoot>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="rrd-card">
  <h2>By priority</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Priority</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Issues</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for bucket in by_priority.buckets %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ bucket.label }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ bucket.count }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

{% comment %} A table long enough to cross a page keeps its header on every page, and a
   `.rrd-card` is never split down the middle. Neither needs anything from you — the report
   stylesheet does both, in the HTML preview and in the PDF. {% endcomment %}
<p class="rrd-muted">Counted in SQL over the issues this report's reader may see.</p>
